that our Rack App

01:13:56.600 --> 01:13:58.600
which is the backend.

01:14:00.600 --> 01:14:02.600
Till now how we were

01:14:02.600 --> 01:14:04.600
using it, interacting with it

01:14:04.600 --> 01:14:06.600
using the code.

01:14:06.600 --> 01:14:08.600
We were writing our query in the code

01:14:08.600 --> 01:14:10.600
and we were getting output on the terminal.

01:14:10.600 --> 01:14:12.600
Which is not

01:14:12.600 --> 01:14:14.600
a mature way to use the application.

01:14:14.600 --> 01:14:16.600
We are directly using our backend.

01:14:16.600 --> 01:14:18.600
We are able to do this because we

01:14:18.600 --> 01:14:20.600
made the application. But what if

01:14:20.600 --> 01:14:22.600
someone else had made the application?

01:14:22.600 --> 01:14:24.600
Or this application that I made, you want to use it.

01:14:24.600 --> 01:14:26.600
So you cannot go into my application

01:14:26.600 --> 01:14:28.600
and pass queries here.

01:14:28.600 --> 01:14:30.600
So what do I have to do?

01:14:30.600 --> 01:14:32.600
I have to give you access here.

01:14:32.600 --> 01:14:34.600
For that access

01:14:34.600 --> 01:14:36.600
I will build an API around my

01:14:36.600 --> 01:14:38.600
application. And you

01:14:38.600 --> 01:14:40.600
through this API, through the internet

01:14:40.600 --> 01:14:42.600
you will access my app here.

01:14:42.600 --> 01:14:44.600
Okay. Now if

01:14:44.600 --> 01:14:46.600
you are able to access this app through the internet

01:14:46.600 --> 01:14:48.600
then what will you do

01:14:48.600 --> 01:14:50.600
you will try to improve this terminal access

01:14:50.600 --> 01:14:52.600
a little bit.

01:14:52.600 --> 01:14:54.600
So you will make a front end for this.

01:14:54.600 --> 01:14:56.600
So in the

01:14:56.600 --> 01:14:58.600
coming lectures we will

01:14:58.600 --> 01:15:00.600
make a front end here. Which will be

01:15:00.600 --> 01:15:02.600
a streamlit front end.

01:15:02.600 --> 01:15:04.600
Where basically

01:15:04.600 --> 01:15:06.600
we will be able to interact with our

01:15:06.600 --> 01:15:08.600
rag application through a UI.

01:15:08.600 --> 01:15:10.600
This terminal interaction

01:15:10.600 --> 01:15:12.600
we will continue it

01:15:12.600 --> 01:15:14.600
and in a proper way

01:15:14.600 --> 01:15:16.600
we will interact with our application.

01:15:16.600 --> 01:15:18.600
Like a normal user does.

01:15:18.600 --> 01:15:20.600
Now here comes the question

01:15:20.600 --> 01:15:22.600
why only fast API?

01:15:22.600 --> 01:15:24.600
And is fast API

01:15:24.600 --> 01:15:26.600
the only way to build

01:15:26.600 --> 01:15:28.600
these APIs? The answer is no.

01:15:28.600 --> 01:15:30.600
Earlier

01:15:30.600 --> 01:15:32.600
when there was no fast API

01:15:32.600 --> 01:15:34.600
then here you used

01:15:34.600 --> 01:15:36.600
libraries like Flask or Django.

01:15:36.600 --> 01:15:38.600
I hope you have heard

01:15:38.600 --> 01:15:40.600
their name.

01:15:40.600 --> 01:15:42.600
These also come in use to make your APIs.

01:15:42.600 --> 01:15:44.600
But Flask

01:15:44.600 --> 01:15:46.600
and Django APIs that you create

01:15:46.600 --> 01:15:48.600
those are your synchronous APIs.

01:15:50.600 --> 01:15:52.600
Synchronous means

01:15:52.600 --> 01:15:54.600
that your server

01:15:56.600 --> 01:15:58.600
can process

01:15:58.600 --> 01:16:00.600
a single request at a time.

01:16:00.600 --> 01:16:02.600
Means you have

01:16:02.600 --> 01:16:04.600
a single waiter

01:16:04.600 --> 01:16:06.600
for the entire restaurant.

01:16:06.600 --> 01:16:08.600
So what will the waiter do

01:16:08.600 --> 01:16:10.600
one particular customer

01:16:10.600 --> 01:16:12.600
let's say customer A

01:16:12.600 --> 01:16:14.600
will take his order

01:16:14.600 --> 01:16:16.600
he will go to the

01:16:16.600 --> 01:16:18.600
kitchen and give the order

01:16:18.600 --> 01:16:20.600
and wait.

01:16:20.600 --> 01:16:22.600
How long will he wait?

01:16:22.600 --> 01:16:24.600
Till the time this particular order is not ready.

01:16:24.600 --> 01:16:26.600
And as soon as it is ready

01:16:26.600 --> 01:16:28.600
he will give this particular order back

01:16:28.600 --> 01:16:30.600
to customer A.

01:16:30.600 --> 01:16:32.600
But at this time it is possible that

01:16:32.600 --> 01:16:34.600
in your restaurant there are customer B

01:16:34.600 --> 01:16:36.600
customer C, D, many customers

01:16:36.600 --> 01:16:38.600
what they will have to do

01:16:38.600 --> 01:16:40.600
they will have to wait here.

01:16:40.600 --> 01:16:42.600
They will have to wait in a particular

01:16:42.600 --> 01:16:44.600
queue and order.

01:16:44.600 --> 01:16:46.600
Means first come first serve.

01:16:46.600 --> 01:16:48.600
Till the time your customer A demand

01:16:48.600 --> 01:16:50.600
is not fully served

01:16:50.600 --> 01:16:52.600
his request is not fully served

01:16:52.600 --> 01:16:54.600
you will not move forward.

01:16:54.600 --> 01:16:56.600
So this is called

01:16:56.600 --> 01:16:58.600
your synchronous way.

01:16:58.600 --> 01:17:00.600
What is asynchronous way?

01:17:02.600 --> 01:17:04.600
Generally people

01:17:04.600 --> 01:17:06.600
get confused in asynchronous

01:17:06.600 --> 01:17:08.600
they think it is a parallel way.

01:17:08.600 --> 01:17:10.600
It is not parallel. What is parallel?

01:17:10.600 --> 01:17:12.600
Parallel means

01:17:12.600 --> 01:17:14.600
this waiter is listening to

01:17:14.600 --> 01:17:16.600
customer A, B, C and D at the same time.

01:17:16.600 --> 01:17:18.600
This is parallel.

01:17:18.600 --> 01:17:20.600
But generally

01:17:20.600 --> 01:17:22.600
asynchronous programming or asynchronous

01:17:22.600 --> 01:17:24.600
servers are not parallel.

01:17:24.600 --> 01:17:26.600
Here another word

01:17:26.600 --> 01:17:28.600
is used that is your concurrent.

01:17:28.600 --> 01:17:30.600
There is a big difference between both.

01:17:30.600 --> 01:17:32.600
Concurrent is like parallel

01:17:32.600 --> 01:17:34.600
but not parallel.

01:17:34.600 --> 01:17:36.600
Parallel means at the same time

01:17:36.600 --> 01:17:38.600
your waiter is listening to

01:17:38.600 --> 01:17:40.600
customer A, B, C and D at the same time.

01:17:40.600 --> 01:17:42.600
Is it possible

01:17:42.600 --> 01:17:44.600
that one person can listen to

01:17:44.600 --> 01:17:46.600
the request of these four people at the same time?

01:17:46.600 --> 01:17:48.600
It is not possible.

01:17:48.600 --> 01:17:50.600
What happens in concurrency?

01:17:50.600 --> 01:17:52.600
A smart approach is used.

01:17:52.600 --> 01:17:54.600
What is smart approach?

01:17:54.600 --> 01:17:56.600
Whenever your waiter

01:17:56.600 --> 01:17:58.600
listen carefully guys it is very important

01:17:58.600 --> 01:18:00.600
Whenever your waiter is free

01:18:00.600 --> 01:18:02.600
then he will

01:18:02.600 --> 01:18:04.600
engage with other customer.

01:18:06.600 --> 01:18:08.600
He first took order of A

01:18:08.600 --> 01:18:10.600
and gave it to customer A in kitchen.

01:18:12.600 --> 01:18:14.600
Now it will take time

01:18:14.600 --> 01:18:16.600
for his dish to get ready in kitchen.

01:18:16.600 --> 01:18:18.600
He will not wait outside the kitchen

01:18:18.600 --> 01:18:20.600
that when A's dish will come

01:18:20.600 --> 01:18:22.600
I will bring A's dish and give it to customer A

01:18:22.600 --> 01:18:24.600
then I will go to customer B.

01:18:24.600 --> 01:18:26.600
He will utilize this time

01:18:26.600 --> 01:18:28.600
and go to customer B.

01:18:28.600 --> 01:18:30.600
He will take B's order and give it to customer B.

01:18:32.600 --> 01:18:34.600
Again B's order

01:18:34.600 --> 01:18:36.600
and one order is getting ready in kitchen.

01:18:36.600 --> 01:18:38.600
Again there is time

01:18:38.600 --> 01:18:40.600
he will go to customer C.

01:18:40.600 --> 01:18:42.600
When he went to customer C

01:18:42.600 --> 01:18:44.600
he took his order and went to kitchen.

01:18:44.600 --> 01:18:46.600
He saw that B's order is prepared.

01:18:46.600 --> 01:18:48.600
It is possible that B ordered coffee

01:18:48.600 --> 01:18:50.600
and it got ready quickly.

01:18:50.600 --> 01:18:52.600
Instead of going to customer D

01:18:52.600 --> 01:18:54.600
he will take B's order and

01:18:54.600 --> 01:18:56.600
will get it to customer B.

01:18:56.600 --> 01:18:58.600
So B is free here.

01:18:58.600 --> 01:19:00.600
He got his order.

01:19:00.600 --> 01:19:02.600
Then it will go to D.

01:19:02.600 --> 01:19:04.600
He will take it to the kitchen.

01:19:04.600 --> 01:19:06.600
As soon as he went to kitchen

01:19:06.600 --> 01:19:08.600
he saw that A's order is ready.

01:19:08.600 --> 01:19:10.600
So he will take A's order.

01:19:10.600 --> 01:19:12.600
A is free.

01:19:12.600 --> 01:19:14.600
Then let's say D is ready.

01:19:14.600 --> 01:19:16.600
So D is free and C is free.

01:19:16.600 --> 01:19:18.600
So here he is not doing many things at one time.

01:19:18.600 --> 01:19:20.600
He is dealing with one order

01:19:20.600 --> 01:19:22.600
and one request.

01:19:22.600 --> 01:19:24.600
But he is using a smart approach

01:19:24.600 --> 01:19:26.600
that is your concurrency.

01:19:26.600 --> 01:19:28.600
When it is empty

01:19:28.600 --> 01:19:30.600
he has no work

01:19:30.600 --> 01:19:32.600
and he is processing it in such a way

01:19:32.600 --> 01:19:34.600
that if in between

01:19:34.600 --> 01:19:36.600
a response of a previous request

01:19:36.600 --> 01:19:38.600
comes

01:19:38.600 --> 01:19:40.600
then he doesn't wait

01:19:40.600 --> 01:19:42.600
that I will process all the requests

01:19:42.600 --> 01:19:44.600
and then I will go to the response.

01:19:44.600 --> 01:19:46.600
No. As the thing that will come in front

01:19:46.600 --> 01:19:48.600
he will do it in that order.

01:19:48.600 --> 01:19:50.600
If the response of any particular request

01:19:50.600 --> 01:19:52.600
is ready, then he will

01:19:52.600 --> 01:19:54.600
take it and give it back.

01:19:54.600 --> 01:19:56.600
That is your concurrency.

01:19:56.600 --> 01:19:58.600
And it is a very important concept.

01:19:58.600 --> 01:20:00.600
It is not your parallelism.

01:20:00.600 --> 01:20:02.600
